{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1624 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3c766d361c8d09eada50c31811acb57356a635fae6a95b8a0ff322c709fe66d1" :edb/db-before-t 1623 :edb/db-after-t 1624 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534936 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:27:13.814Z" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119516 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119516 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119516 1145 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= -det(A) \\\\\n&= - (-16) \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119516 1145 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-16) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119517 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119517 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119517 1145 "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= \\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 32 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119517 1145 "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\dfrac{1}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119518 1100 "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$, $R_6:= 4 R_6$, and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$. What is the value of $\\det(B)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119518 1100 "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5,$ $R_6:= 4 R_6,$ and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7.$ What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119518 1145 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119518 1145 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4$, so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row, so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= 4 \\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\\n&=-4det(A) \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119589 1100 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119589 1100 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/assets/3692f985d592f9680ca9fbf38991df32c7665ae02c7dae04fec6062e3b62884d.png)" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119589 1145 "Since\n\n$$\n\\det(X)=\\det(Y)\n$$\n\n, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form\n\n$$\nR_j:= R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(X) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + 3R_{3} \\\\ =\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= - 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= det(Y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3\n$$" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119589 1145 "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119590 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5$. What is the value of $\\det(2A)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119590 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5.$ What is the value of $\\det(2A)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119590 1145 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k$, and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\ndet(k \\cdot A) = k^{n} \\cdot det(A)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(2A) &= 2^{4} \\cdot det(A) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 false] [17592186119590 1145 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186119699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320887 13194139534936 true] [17592186119699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320894 13194139534936 true] [17592186119699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320992 13194139534936 true] [17592186119699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320999 13194139534936 true] [17592186119700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320901 13194139534936 true] [17592186119700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320908 13194139534936 true] [17592186119700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320971 13194139534936 true] [17592186119700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321006 13194139534936 true] [17592186119701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320936 13194139534936 true] [17592186119701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320957 13194139534936 true] [17592186119701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320978 13194139534936 true] [17592186119701 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321027 13194139534936 true] [17592186119771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320943 13194139534936 true] [17592186119771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320950 13194139534936 true] [17592186119771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320964 13194139534936 true] [17592186119771 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320985 13194139534936 true] [17592186119772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320915 13194139534936 true] [17592186119772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320922 13194139534936 true] [17592186119772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320929 13194139534936 true] [17592186119772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321013 13194139534936 true] [17592186119772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321020 13194139534936 true] [17592186120347 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534936 true] [17592186120347 1100 "Given that $det(A) = det(B)$, which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186120347 1100 "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120347 1145 "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534936 true] [17592186120359 1100 "Suppose $U$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix and $det(U) = 10$. The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_{2}:=R_{2} + 6R_{4}$ and $R_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{3}$. What is the value of $det(V)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186120359 1100 "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=10.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120359 1145 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{2},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{2}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534936 true] [17592186120380 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\cdot 6$ matrix and $det(A) = 35$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{5}R_{1}$. What is the value of $det(B)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186120380 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(A)=35.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120380 1145 "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-\\frac{3}{5}det(A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120381 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534936 true] [17592186120381 1100 "Suppose $X$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix and $det(X) = 105$. The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_{3}↔R_{1}$ (switching the first and the third rows). What is the value of $det(Y)$?" 13194139534936 false] [17592186120381 1100 "Suppose $X$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(X)=105.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}$ (switching the first and the third rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186120381 1145 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(105) \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320887 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320888 13194139534936 true] [17592186320887 1089 "q-130678" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320887 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534936 true] [17592186320887 1100 "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=-15.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ (switching the first and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320887 1113 #uuid "db9c61a2-e798-54a1-8721-392ffe488157" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320887 1145 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-15) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320889 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320890 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320891 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320892 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320893 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1041 "selection" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320890 13194139534936 true] [17592186320888 1110 #uuid "1f2f26ea-222c-5966-b3a1-7ade3a02c12c" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320889 1034 "-15" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320889 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534936 true] [17592186320889 1111 #uuid "d21bbdf5-748d-5858-8760-4a146bf53774" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320890 1034 "15" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534936 true] [17592186320890 1111 #uuid "f325b305-63bf-555a-a0ee-6e2cb58e5233" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320891 1034 "17" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534936 true] [17592186320891 1111 #uuid "212dee85-1ebc-5991-ae48-991734716a90" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320892 1034 "Undefined" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534936 true] [17592186320892 1111 #uuid "fe874a82-4003-5a81-a584-8be64aac0276" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320893 1034 "0" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534936 true] [17592186320893 1111 #uuid "b1a7aab9-c015-5f9c-ac57-0973c3f676fc" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320894 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320895 13194139534936 true] [17592186320894 1089 "q-130682" 13194139534936 true] [17592186320894 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534936 true] [17592186320894 1100 "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=55.$ The matrix $Y$ is obt